{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 30, :comments ["<b>And My soul shall abhor you, etc.</b> One needs to know why it was necessary to say this, for it is obvious that His soul would abhor them — since at the time when they are perfect tzaddikim they were blessed that His soul would not abhor them (Leviticus 26:11), and it is self-evident that this blessing would not be in effect during the days of their wickedness. And if the intent is to state the reversal of the blessings, He should have stated the reversal of all the others as well. Rather, it is certain that He said only the punishments that are in addition to the absence of the blessings." "It appears that the verse intends to say that even relative to the tzaddikim who would exist at that time, My soul would abhor them — in the mystical sense of (Hosea 4:5) ״וְכָשַׁל גַּם נָבִיא עִמְּךָ״. It further intends that prophets would no longer prophesy in Israel, for HaShem's Shekhinah — which is ascribed to them in the aspect of soul — would not shine upon them. And this curse is no curse greater than it, and therefore He fixed it after several curses. From this you learn that it is greater than those preceding it, and it is the one that has been fulfilled in our time, and for it our eyes have failed and our soul has languished exceedingly — woe unto us."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:02:21.823319838Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}